ZERO STUDIO GUEST EDITOR VERIFICATION
2026-11-18

Candidate tested
/tmp/zero-support/validation-0.13/integration/build-third/zero.iso
SHA256 45ce79a877fc7a47d105217a2631cc79d6106f2a781619e718859fbad01d50d2
This report covers the guest editor in that candidate, not later untested builds.

Original Zero native checks: 35 passed
28 event/render checks cover insertion, middle insertion, Backspace/Delete,
Home/End/Up/Down, exact 400-byte capacity, refusing extra input without changing
source, unsaved-close/open/new confirmations, filename entry, Save dispatch,
compiler dispatch, language selection, canvas bounds and caret bounds.
7 real cooperative file-service client checks use the original fork/switch and
filesystem service. They cover existing reads, missing files as unsaved new
buffers, invalid-path preservation, 400-byte reads, 401-byte rejection, NUL/binary
rejection and Save rejection for an unavailable filesystem. No fake successful
disk write was substituted in these checks.
Sources: guest-check-head/tail.zero.part; guest-file-check-head/tail.zero.part.
Native execution artifacts: /tmp/zero-support/validation-0.13/editor-guest.

Actual guest execution: BIOS64 and BIOS32
Each mode passed 20 editor-state assertions and 12 terminal command checks,
including 4 cold-boot file checks.
The tests used independent 16MiB disks, real PS/2 keyboard events and the actual
GUI frame/state. The editor opened an existing Zero source file, moved the caret
with End/Left, deleted and inserted a character, compiled and ran the changed
program with the bundled Zero compiler, then saved it through the real disk
service. A new Ezero buffer compiled and ran through the bundled frontend,
packer and Zero compiler, and Save As created its file on disk.
Both modes exercised unsaved-close and unsaved-open protection, cancellation,
failed-open preservation, 400-byte overflow rejection and invalid Zero compiler
diagnostics without losing source. The original source, Ezero source, 400-byte
file and independent sentinel all survived a full VM stop and fresh boot.

Actual guest execution: UEFI64 and UEFI32
Both modes passed GUI Zero compilation/execution, Ezero compilation/execution,
Save, New, language selection and Save As. The saved Zero/Ezero files and
sentinel were read again after a full VM stop and fresh boot.
All guest tests used QEMU with BIOS or OVMF firmware. They are not a claim of a
separate VirtualBox hardware test or support for every physical machine.

Visual evidence
Actual 1920x1080 framebuffer captures were encoded and validated with the
original Zero picture codec. These are running-system screenshots, not mockups:
/tmp/zero-support/validation-0.13/browser-vm/editor-third-bios64/editor-ezero.zpix
/tmp/zero-support/validation-0.13/browser-vm/editor-third-bios32/editor-ezero.zpix
The code, insertion caret, saved state and actual Ezero program output are
visible. The candidate retained an incorrect inner-window WiFi title; root was
notified for correction independently of the editor state/content renderer.

Serial test infrastructure finding
The early external VM harness stopped draining its serial socket during HMP
keyboard/screenshot operations. Per-character GUI frame mirroring could then
fill the socket and trigger the guest's bounded UART timeout. The external
harness now drains in a background reader. The editor also now mirrors only
bounded status/diagnostic transitions, avoiding full-frame output per keystroke.
No UART implementation change was needed for these tests.

Protected user data remained unchanged
